<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

        <!--
          The namespace is Vixen.App and the assembly is not, which is the whole point of the split.
          Consumers keep writing `using Vixen.App;` and referencing the Vixen.App package; what
          changed is that the frame code behind it is compiled under the RUNTIME profile — AOT- and
          trim-analyzed, API-baselined, documentation-gated, and rooted in Tools/Vixen.AotProbe —
          while the two assemblies that pick a backend stay above Platform/ where they have to.
        -->
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.App</RootNamespace>

        <Description>
            The application host: the boot sequence, the frame loop, the services a game is handed
            and the join between a world and a drawn frame. Everything Vixen.App does except
            choosing which graphics backend and which platform to open.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>host;bootstrap;game-loop;app;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Diagnostics\Vixen.Core.Diagnostics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.IO\Vixen.Core.IO.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />

        <!--
          The content the application loads. A host that could not open a catalog left every game to
          stand up the catalog, the bundle source and the manager itself, which is what Samples/07
          does by hand and what a game should not have to.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Assets\Vixen.Assets.csproj" />

        <!--
          The world, the systems, the behaviours and the fixed-step accumulator. A Game with a world
          is what this host exists for, so it builds one unless AppConfig.UseEngine says otherwise —
          which is what a tool head, a server with its own loop, or a UI-only app head says.

          Note what this is not: it is not a licence for Vixen.Ui to reference Vixen.Engine. That
          boundary is about Vixen.Ui and CheckArchitecture still enforces it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Engine\Vixen.Engine.csproj" />

        <!--
          The frame. WorldRenderer is the join between a world and a drawn frame — the standard
          features, the shared geometry buffer, the extraction systems and a SceneRenderHost — and
          until this reference existed nothing outside a test project constructed one, so every
          sample built the whole stack by hand and none of them was a game.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Engine.Renderer\Vixen.Engine.Renderer.csproj" />

        <!--
          The quality waterfall. AppGraphics resolves GraphicsOptions.Quality into the numbers a
          frame's budgets are set from, which is a thing only a host can do: the tier is the
          platform's pick and the project's preset is an asset somebody loaded, and neither is
          knowable from inside the compositor. What that resolution feeds is TerrainFactory —
          Vixen.Rendering.Terrain cannot reference this assembly, so the host is the only object
          that can see both ends of that hand-off.

          Note what this does *not* license: the node kinds in here still reach a document through
          GraphicsOptions.Factories, because CompositorBuilder is the one that cannot switch on a
          type downstream of it. That reference cycle is unaffected by this one.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Rendering.PostFx\Vixen.Rendering.PostFx.csproj" />

        <!-- The action system. Named here rather than taken transitively through Vixen.Engine,
             because PlatformInput is what joins it to Vixen.Platform and that seam is this
             assembly's own — see PlatformInput.cs. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Input\Vixen.Input.csproj" />

        <!--
          The platform contracts — windows, surfaces, lifecycle, input devices. Reachable from here
          only because they are in Core/: they are a contract, not an implementation, and doc 02's
          rule for the Platform/ folder is about implementations. See the note in Vixen.slnx.

          ⚠ What is deliberately *not* here: Vixen.Platform.Desktop, Vixen.Platform.Headless,
          Vixen.Graphics.Vulkan and Vixen.Graphics.Null. Deciding between them is what
          IPlatformFactory and IGraphicsBackend are asked, and Tools/Vixen.App answers.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Platform\Vixen.Platform.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.App.Tests" />

        <!--
          The other half of this component. Tools/Vixen.App is the same host, split off only because
          picking a backend means referencing Platform/ and this assembly may not — so the two are
          one thing wearing two assembly names, and the boundary between them is an artefact of the
          layering rather than a public surface.

          What it is actually for today is one property: PlatformHost records why it fell back to
          the headless platform in AppConfig.HeadlessFallbackReason, whose setter is internal
          because nothing outside the boot path has any business writing it. Making that setter
          public to avoid this line would be a worse trade — a knob on every AppConfig, so that one
          assembly could say something only it can know.
        -->
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.App" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
